> Here’s a compact briefing for the visit—focused on who they are, overlap with your seminar, and good conversation hooks.
> 
> | Person | What to know / likely angle | Good conversation hooks |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Jeffrey E. Sun** | Assistant Professor of Economics, U of T; climate economics, household impacts of climate change, computational economic modeling. Joined U of T in 2024. Breakfast/dinner organizer, so likely one of your main hosts. | Climate impacts at household vs macro scale; computational methods; heterogeneity and adaptation in climate damage functions. |
> | **Jacquelyn Pless** | Assistant Professor of Strategic Management at Rotman. Works on innovation, energy/environmental economics, and public finance. | Clean-tech innovation, energy policy design, R&D subsidies, how firm behavior mediates decarbonization. |
> | **Christian Spielmann** | Professor of Economics at U of T as of July 2026; interests include economics education and environmental economics. Previously linked to University of Bristol. | Teaching climate economics; communicating uncertainty in climate-damage estimates; pedagogy around environmental economics. |
> | **Karen Clay** | Senior economic historian/environmental economist; currently Professor of Economics and Public Policy at Carnegie Mellon, with past U of T connection. Works in energy, environment, pollution, health, and historical political economy. | Long-run pollution damages; historical evidence on adaptation/regulation; using quasi-experimental evidence to inform climate damage estimates. |
> | **Larasati “Lara” Wulandari** | U of T economics PhD student in development/environmental economics. Project: **air pollution and environmental damages—measurement, health effects, and policy**. Lunch organizer and afternoon meeting. | Air pollution measurement; health impacts in developing contexts; links between local pollution and broader climate-development damages. |
> | **Benjamin Lauzon** | Public info is sparse. Appears as an instructor for U of T ECO220, *Introduction to Data Analysis and Applied Econometrics*. Lunch participant. | Applied econometrics, teaching data analysis, empirical identification in climate/economic impact work. |
> | **Ayaka Behro** | U of T economics PhD candidate in macro, financial, and climate economics. Work includes household finance and flood insurance/adaptation, plus a project on the global sand shortage. | Flood risk, adaptation investment, credit/liquidity constraints, household-level climate resilience. |
> | **Eduardo Souza-Rodrigues** | Professor and Associate Chair for Faculty and Research at U of T Mississauga Economics. Works in environmental economics, industrial organization, and econometrics. | Structural models of environmental policy; Amazon/land-use type questions if relevant; econometric challenges in climate impact estimation. |
> | **Hugo Cordeau** | U of T PhD alumnus; now Assistant Professor at Concordia. Environmental and urban economics, transportation, decarbonization. | Urban climate policy, transport decarbonization, spatial heterogeneity in damages/adaptation. |
> | **Christoph Semken** | Assistant Professor of Economics at U of T, started 2025. Environmental, behavioral, and applied economics, including climate economics. Dinner participant. | Behavioral responses to climate risk; adaptation behavior; integrating behavioral frictions into damage estimates. |
> 
> **Overall read of the day:** this is a climate/environment-heavy audience with strong empirical, applied micro, and policy interests. For your seminar on **“Economic disparities in a warming world: a meta-analysis of GDP impacts,”** expect especially good questions on heterogeneity, identification, adaptation, aggregation from micro to macro, and how GDP impacts relate to welfare, health, and distributional damages.
